/*
Key idea:
For a graph H satisfying:
1) It is a supergraph of the given tree G (simple graph),
2) diam(H) = diam(G) = D,
3) For every non-edge xy in H, adding xy decreases the diameter,

such H is exactly a maximal graph among graphs on the same vertex set with diameter D containing G.

For a tree G, let its diameter path be considered.

Facts:
- If the tree has a unique center (D even), then every valid H is uniquely determined:
  vertices are partitioned by their branch from the center and depth.
  Two vertices can be connected iff their distance in G is < D.
  This yields exactly 1 graph.

- If the tree has two centers c1-c2 (D odd, let k=(D-1)/2), then:
  remove the center edge, getting two components L and R rooted at c1 and c2.
  Let A_i = number of vertices in L at distance i from c1, for i=0..k
      B_i = number of vertices in R at distance i from c2, for i=0..k
  (A_k, B_k > 0 due to diameter endpoints)

  Inside each side, all allowable edges are forced (distance < D always within same side).
  Across sides, a pair (u in L depth i, v in R depth j) may be added iff i+j < k.
  Pairs with i+j > k cannot be added (would reduce diameter below D immediately).
  Pairs with i+j = k are optional, but to satisfy maximality condition, every such non-edge must,
  when added, reduce the diameter. This happens iff after adding all chosen optional edges,
  for every deepest pair (x in L depth k, y in R depth k), there exists a chosen optional edge
  on some path shortcuting x-y to length D-1. Equivalently, if we define:
     S_L = set of depths i such that some vertex at depth i in L is incident to a chosen optional edge
     S_R similarly on R,
  then for every deepest x and y we need some i in S_L, j in S_R with i+j = k.
  Since chosen optional edges are between exact depth layers i and k-i, and all vertices in a layer
  are symmetric, the condition becomes:
     Let T be the set of i (0..k) for which at least one edge is chosen between layer A_i and B_{k-i}.
     Then T must be non-empty.
  Moreover, choices for different i are independent, and for fixed i any subset of the
  A_i * B_{k-i} possible edges may be chosen, except not all i can choose empty simultaneously.

  Therefore answer = product_i (2^(A_i * B_{k-i})) - 1, for i=0..k.

This matches small examples:
- Path of 4 vertices: k=1, layers sizes [1,1] and [1,1], answer=(2^1 * 2^1)-1=3.
- Star: unique center => 1.

Implementation:
1) Read tree.
2) Find diameter endpoints by two BFS.
3) Recover diameter path.
4) If D even => print 1.
5) Else:
   centers are middle two vertices on diameter path.
   BFS from each center avoiding the other center to count layer sizes up to k.
   Compute product of 2^(A_i*B_{k-i}) mod MOD, subtract 1.

Complexity: O(N).
*/

public class Main {
    static final long MOD = 998244353L;

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        FastScanner fs = new FastScanner(System.in);
        int N = fs.nextInt();
        List<Integer>[] g = new ArrayList[N];
        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) g[i] = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < N - 1; i++) {
            int a = fs.nextInt() - 1;
            int b = fs.nextInt() - 1;
            g[a].add(b);
            g[b].add(a);
        }

        int s = bfsFarthest(0, g, null).node;
        BFSRes res1 = bfsFarthest(s, g, null);
        int t = res1.node;
        int[] parent = res1.parent;
        int D = res1.dist[t];

        List<Integer> path = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int cur = t; cur != -1; cur = parent[cur]) path.add(cur);
        Collections.reverse(path);

        if (D % 2 == 0) {
